WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.844
Diffusion is mass transport by atomic motion.

2
00:00:03.844 --> 00:00:09.523
It is how we case harden steel gears, dope silicon wafers, and chrome plate parts.

3
00:00:12.643 --> 00:00:15.352
Self diffusion happens in pure elements.

4
00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:20.656
Interdiffusion happens between two materials, driven by a concentration gradient.

5
00:00:20.656 --> 00:00:25.844
Your lecture's diffusion couple: copper and nickel bars, joined and heated.

6
00:00:26.264 --> 00:00:29.445
At high temperature, atoms jump across the interface in

7
00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:32.572
both directions, until the two metals intermix.

8
00:00:32.992 --> 00:00:35.189
Heat makes atoms vibrate harder.

9
00:00:35.189 --> 00:00:37.724
When the temperature is high enough, they

10
00:00:37.724 --> 00:00:40.709
overcome the energy barriers to atomic motion.

11
00:00:40.709 --> 00:00:46.004
And substitutional diffusion needs vacancies, which give the atoms somewhere to go.

12
00:00:49.904 --> 00:00:53.222
Two mechanisms. First, vacancy diffusion.

13
00:00:53.222 --> 00:00:55.922
An atom jumps into a neighboring vacancy.

14
00:00:55.922 --> 00:01:00.534
Watch: the atoms move one way, and the vacancy moves the other way.

15
00:01:00.954 --> 00:01:05.462
The number of vacancies follows the Arrhenius equation from topic five.

16
00:01:05.462 --> 00:01:10.722
Higher temperature means more vacancies and more energy, so diffusion is faster.

17
00:01:11.142 --> 00:01:13.444
Second, interstitial diffusion.

18
00:01:13.444 --> 00:01:18.649
A small atom, like carbon in iron, jumps from one interstitial site to the next.

19
00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:21.828
It is usually much faster, because there are many more

20
00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:25.225
empty interstitial sites, and no vacancy is needed.

21
00:01:29.325 --> 00:01:32.930
To put numbers on diffusion, define the flux, J: the

22
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.085
mass diffusing through a unit area per unit time.

23
00:01:36.505 --> 00:01:39.843
If the flux does not change with time, the diffusion

24
00:01:39.843 --> 00:01:45.446
is steady state, and Fick's first law applies. J equals minus D, d C dx.

25
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:50.080
D is the diffusion coefficient, in square meters per second.

26
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.598
The minus sign means atoms move down the concentration gradient, from high to low.

27
00:01:56.017 --> 00:01:59.130
In steady state the profile is a straight line.

28
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.741
The slope is the same everywhere, so the flux in equals the flux out.

29
00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:08.698
A gas diffusing through a metal plate at constant pressure is the classic case.

30
00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:17.278
Your lecture's example: purifying hydrogen through a palladium sheet.

31
00:02:17.278 --> 00:02:20.087
Five millimeters thick, area zero point two

32
00:02:20.087 --> 00:02:23.553
square meters, at five hundred degrees Celsius.

33
00:02:23.553 --> 00:02:28.572
D is one times ten to the minus eight square meters per second, and the hydrogen

34
00:02:28.572 --> 00:02:33.711
concentrations are two point four and zero point six kilograms per cubic meter.

35
00:02:34.132 --> 00:02:38.668
The flux is D times the concentration difference over the thickness.

36
00:02:38.668 --> 00:02:41.510
One times ten to the minus eight, times one

37
00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.837
point eight, over zero point zero zero five.

38
00:02:44.837 --> 00:02:47.619
That is three point six times ten to the minus

39
00:02:47.619 --> 00:02:50.582
six kilograms per square meter per second.

40
00:02:51.002 --> 00:02:53.897
Mass equals flux times area times time.

41
00:02:53.897 --> 00:02:57.232
Three point six times ten to the minus six, times

42
00:02:57.232 --> 00:03:01.008
zero point two, times thirty six hundred seconds.

43
00:03:01.008 --> 00:03:04.154
That is two point six times ten to the minus three

44
00:03:04.154 --> 00:03:08.496
kilograms, about two point six grams of hydrogen per hour.

45
00:03:12.295 --> 00:03:15.675
In most real situations, diffusion is not steady.

46
00:03:15.675 --> 00:03:19.561
The flux and the concentration gradient both change with time.

47
00:03:19.561 --> 00:03:21.702
Then we need Fick's second law.

48
00:03:22.122 --> 00:03:27.372
A useful case is a semi infinite solid: a bar long enough that the diffusing atoms

49
00:03:27.372 --> 00:03:32.316
never reach the far end, with the surface concentration held constant.

50
00:03:32.316 --> 00:03:35.551
Then the profile moves deeper as time goes on.

51
00:03:35.971 --> 00:03:40.796
The solution is: C x minus C zero, over C s minus C zero,

52
00:03:40.796 --> 00:03:45.412
equals one minus the error function of x over two root D t.

53
00:03:45.832 --> 00:03:49.897
C s is the constant surface concentration, C zero the initial

54
00:03:49.897 --> 00:03:54.650
concentration, and C x the concentration at depth x after time t.

55
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.840
The error function values come from a table.

56
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.092
This is exactly how case hardening works: carbon

57
00:04:01.092 --> 00:04:04.156
diffuses into the surface of a steel gear.

58
00:04:07.956 --> 00:04:11.086
Your lecture's example: decarburization.

59
00:04:11.086 --> 00:04:14.769
An F C C iron carbon alloy with zero point three five weight

60
00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:18.819
percent carbon is held at eleven twenty seven degrees Celsius,

61
00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:22.624
in an atmosphere that keeps the surface at zero carbon.

62
00:04:22.624 --> 00:04:27.412
Where is the carbon zero point one five weight percent after ten hours?

63
00:04:27.412 --> 00:04:30.910
D is six point nine times ten to the minus eleven.

64
00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:36.114
The left side is zero point one five minus zero point three five, over

65
00:04:36.114 --> 00:04:41.027
zero minus zero point three five: zero point five seven one four.

66
00:04:41.027 --> 00:04:46.910
So the error function of z equals one minus that, zero point four two eight six.

67
00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.077
From the table, the error function of zero

68
00:04:50.077 --> 00:04:53.183
point four is zero point four two eight four.

69
00:04:53.183 --> 00:04:55.333
So z is zero point four zero.

70
00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:58.091
z equals x over two root D t.

71
00:04:58.091 --> 00:05:01.857
Root D t is the square root of six point nine times ten to

72
00:05:01.857 --> 00:05:06.012
the minus eleven, times thirty six thousand seconds: one

73
00:05:06.012 --> 00:05:09.908
point five eight times ten to the minus three meters.

74
00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:15.557
So x is two times zero point four times that: one point two six millimeters.

75
00:05:19.457 --> 00:05:21.659
What makes diffusion faster?

76
00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:24.742
Smaller atoms squeeze through more easily.

77
00:05:24.742 --> 00:05:29.711
Open lattices like B C C allow faster diffusion than close packed F C C.

78
00:05:29.711 --> 00:05:33.549
And hosts with lower melting points have weaker bonds.

79
00:05:33.969 --> 00:05:39.444
But temperature has the strongest effect, through another Arrhenius equation.

80
00:05:39.444 --> 00:05:43.302
D equals D zero, times e to the minus Q d over R T.

81
00:05:43.723 --> 00:05:49.306
D zero is a pre exponential constant, Q d the activation energy for diffusion,

82
00:05:49.306 --> 00:05:54.499
and R the gas constant, eight point three one joules per mole kelvin.

83
00:05:54.499 --> 00:05:58.200
Take logs, and log D against one over T is a straight

84
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.835
line with slope minus Q d over two point three R.

85
00:06:02.254 --> 00:06:06.173
So, from your lecture's summary: diffusion is faster in open

86
00:06:06.173 --> 00:06:10.438
structures, in lower melting point materials, in materials held by

87
00:06:10.438 --> 00:06:14.876
secondary bonds rather than covalent bonds, and for smaller atoms.

88
00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:20.765
Here is the summary of topic seven.

89
00:06:21.184 --> 00:06:26.769
Atoms diffuse by vacancy or interstitial jumps, and interstitial is usually faster.

90
00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:29.963
In steady state, Fick's first law gives the flux.

91
00:06:30.384 --> 00:06:32.761
When the profile changes with time, use the

92
00:06:32.761 --> 00:06:35.390
error function solution of Fick's second law.

93
00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.682
And D rises exponentially with temperature.

94
00:06:38.682 --> 00:06:42.761
Next, how metals respond to load: mechanical properties.
